{\*\generator Msftedit 5.41.21.2510;}\viewkind4\uc1\pard\sl276\slmult1\lang11\f0\fs22 NVC, Needs/Feelings based AI system\par
\par
\b Summary:\b0\par
\par
In the effort to rekindle whats really at the heart of human communications to change the way we think and interact with one another.\line\par
It is also to have a bottom-up structured solution for AI (Artificial Intelligence) that makes logical sense and is infinitely expandable/flexible for a video game.\line\line All of this is based on NVC - Nonviolent Communication by Marshall Rosenberg.\line\line\line\b A Major Issue:\line\b0\par
Turns out, for the last 10.000 years we, humans, have been living in domination structures which has shaped our culture to speak in a certain way, to think in a certain way, to act in a certain way to people around us. Namely in a rather violent way that causes incredible misery, violence and suffering on the planet.\line\line NVC points this out and proposes a different way of talking, thinking and acting that we actually do posess at a very young age, but it gets stomped on and we are programmed to suppress it. The reasons for this stomping and programming turn out to be quite tragic and suicidal too. Not even the ones doing it are actually benefiting from it, ironically.\par
\par
See NVC material by Marshall Rosenberg (plenty of vids on youtube, books and audio to torrent on this to check out) to understand what I mean in the 2 above paragraphs, I'm not going to go into detail how NVC works here, since I want to focus on its application for AI in project Planet Warfare (PW).\par
\par
The point is, this is a massive issue that is now, has before and can in the future, affect every facet of my life and the lives of everyone else very negatively. The problem is prominent from family, schools, workplaces, sports, hobbies, activities, projects, social and to casual interactions. Pretty much literally everywhere.\par
\par
Art, media and entertainment are massive powerhouses when it comes to influencing the people that consume it, which happens to be everyone by default, so a video game that can become a center focus for people's lives in a very significant way, has tremendous power to change the world.\line\line By building the AI system from a 'needs-feelings' foundation, instead of a 'strategies' one, PW can show a world that works accoding to compassionate and empathic routines, instead of domination and judgemental ones. \par
\par
The effect will be subtle, but immensly powerful because its utilitizing the power of encouragement to the fullest capacity. By being immersed in an environment that works a certain way will rub off onto the inhabitant, thus changing their way of thinking, talking and acting very dramatically, consequently bleeding that effect from the ingame universe in to the real world.\par
\par
\line\b The idea:\b0\line\line The whole idea is two fold. Use NVC to model the AI systems, from a functional perspective as an ingame implementation and use NVC to create an environment that teaches NVC to the player by showing them another way of talking and being talked to by all aspects of the game's communication to the player (user interface, npc behaviour, universe behaviour, story, ingame content, nature of ingame content, nature of how systems, UI, content, tutorials, guides, presentation... how everything is designed etc).\line\line NVC will be woven into the very fabric of the game in all ways there are, literally. Both ingame and outgame. Outgame meaning portals, forums, discussion boards, databases, learning centers... the works. All of them.\line\line In addition to "being the change it wants to be", there needs to be active material to study and learn NVC both within the game and outside of it.\par
\par
Consequently, its equally important to filter and avoid violent language that makes the giant problem worse in all aspects of the game and its content. The environment needs extreme care at the foundation level to not have a trace of encouragement that would perpetuate or reinforce more violence on to the planet.\line\line While the core needs to be handled with high degree of care, the surface is free to be what it wants, since no one can control that. As long as the core foundation is in place, any attempts at violent language will end up painful and counterintuitive to everyone involved, insinuating more NVC-like strategies to be favorable to use over the violent ones.\line\line Domination culture and all that is related to it are to be eradicated. They can appear as cautionary stories and ridiculed exposures that show just how suicidal, stupid and counterintuitive it is, to acknowledge and understand why it is so dangerous, without actually being subjected to it.\line\line\line\b Character/NPC AI:\b0\line\line At the heart of the AI system are NEEDS. Its modeled after the Manfred Maxneef's Matrix of Human Needs, where anything anyone ever does ingame is to meet a need. And anything any character speaks or communicates is Please or Thank You.\line\line Communicating happens through FEELINGS, where depending on the urgency of the need, the context, past occurences and the level of hope/desperation to meet the need, they can start from calm feelings like a smile, thank you, irritation, worry to outright extreme joy, anger, fear and so on.\line\line Each character type has their own way of expressing feelings and default methods to meet the needs behind them, so while humans act in one way, animals, insects, machines, monsters and aliens may do so in radically different ways. They use whatever physical and mental feature they possess.\line\line The AI will discern what needs its currently having unmet, will express a feeling and then think of the simplest methods of satisfying it using its existing knowledge. The opposite is true as well. If the AI has had its need met, it will express a feeling to itself or to the source that met that need and record that data into itself for future reference.\line\line Data recorded is fairly simple.\line -Individuals/Sources or types of individuals/Sources and how they are rated.\line (dangerous, safe, indifferent, unknown)\line -Areas and routes (dangerous, safe, indifferent, unknown, estimated fast or slow).\line -Previous strategies used and the success/failure rate.\line\line Data stored is literally Memory, where each character type has a maximum capacity and each memory has priority, which causes old, low-priority memories to be overwritten by newer memories. Essentially, AI can forget stuff the same way anyone can IRL. As such, information archiving and safekeeping are of huge importance. \par
\par
Things like libraries, vaults, poster/media reminders and datastorages have real value and meaning to exist ingame. Especially for things like crafting blueprints, important information about dangers/benefits of something/anything and for personal/societal Identify Friend/Foe (IFF) systems to remember who is who in the world.\line\line\par
\b Paradigms:\b0\par
\par
When it comes to animals and humans, it seems like there is massive difference. While both are capable of feelings and needs, humans are able to look at a situation from a transcended level, see the bigger picture, influence the future, plan, invent, think out of the box and bypass a lifestyle that animals cannot seem to have any capability to impart from.\line\line Its like animals operate a kin to mechanical machines, very complex ones, but still limited to their environments and natural traits. Meanwhile humans can reshape the environment around them at will, radically changing the world around them and their lives. Its debatable, but I think vaguely that humans have a soul, while animals don't. The soul is the thing that allows the out-of-the-box thinking and intentional, willful, abstact, future manipulation operation that animals do not possess.\line\line The biggest thing that influenced this thought was the story of a lush green island where there were no animals of any kind present, and as an experiment, some people brought deer to this isolated paradise with abundance of food, space and no natural predators. The people dropped them off and returned to the island about 20 years later.\line\line Turns out, with no natural predators and the deer not having foresight, they simply ate, reproduced with no concern for sustainability. After 20 years, the island had become a barren, consumed wasteland, littered with a lot of deer skeletons that obviously had starved to death. The deer ate the food faster than it could regenerate and reproduced with no control, causing more mouths than there was food. The result was mass starvation that wiped out the whole population down to every single deer. None survived.\line\line If predators were present, there would have been an equilibrium, like a rock paper scissors kind of thing, where deer ate grass, wolves ate deer, and grass was allowed to grow sustainably thanks to the wolves keeping the population under control.\line\line In this story, the deer lacked the capability to realize the idea of sustainability. Humans dont have this issue. Humans can predict and foresee the future by looking at current events, making it fairly obvious what might happen if things keep going at the rate they are now. \par
\par
Meta-thinking. Paradigms. Foresight.\par
\par
So as part of the AI system for character types capable of containing a soul, a human for example, they can have the additional AI component of Paradigms.\line\line Things like sustainability, equality (technically thats sustainability too), good/bad or another abstract higher cause(this might as well be sustainability too lol).\line\line Essentially its about overriding the priorities of needs and altering the strategy to meet it. There may be a need for sexual intercourse, but having a paradigm can redivert the strategy of meeting that need in a way that supports the paradigm. In this case, the need for meaning had higher priority than sexual gratification, thus fulfilling the paradigm of sustainable growth of population. (because both extinction and overpopulation is not fun)\line\line Another example is a need for sustenance (hunger) being overriden by a paradigm, to have the need for meaning take over and instead of fully fulfilling the need for sustenance for eating the entire loaf of bread yourself, you instead share it others to ensure the paradigm of equality. (because you know the other person can turn to violence if you dont share)\line\line\line\b Chaos System and the AI:\b0\par
\par
The Chaos system kicks in when something really crazy is happening, something overwhelming, something epic. Something that causes... awe. shock. speechlessness.\par
\par
Basically, the most compatible Chaos operation for AI, that does not affect the quality, fidelity, detail of the intelligence of a character, is to make them simply react slower.\line\line Events thats cause chaos are epic and in the case of AI, it leaves them awestruck, overwhelmed. This causes the AI to greatly extend its delay and calculations on what to do next and how to do it. It also conviniently mirrors real life, as huge events can leave a person paralyzed in the moment, as they try to process what is happening and what to do about it.\par
\par
\line\b The natural delay of AI processes:\b0\line\line As highlighted above, characters have a delay before they figure out what to do and how to do it. In a Chaos event, this delay is extended to lengths that make the AI feel like its overwhelmed and paralyzed.\line\line While figuring out what would be the most effective way to simplify AI behaviour, I noticed that some options I thought of would have bad consequences, thus were unsuitable.\line\line Such ideas were:\line -Simplifying pathfinding\line -Abstracting behaviours for a more simpler alternatives\line -Throwing away logic for more feral instincts\line -Forgetting/Bypassing memories to speed up processing of next action\par
-Grouping multiple same-owner AIs in an area to a single command\line -Kill the AI all together (forced temp paralysis of all AI processes, not just a delay, followed by an AI reboot afterwards)\par
\par
It would have meant that in a Chaos event, character AIs would suddenly do... stupid shit.\line\line Actually wait.\line\line Since a Chaos Event already would cause shock and awe, which is a stupid action already, and during flight or fight situation in real life, humans do stupid shit too... so... huh. Bingo.\par
\par
Except in some cases (busy, but casual city area vs oh my god giant spaceship city crash), certain abstract simplifications/chaos optimizations may not be suitable and would require the chaos event to cause something significant besides just being a casually busy scene.\par
\par
Anyway ok thats cool. I wanted to talk about the delay.\line\line Thing is, in video games the usual scenario is that a single human player is up against millions of AI avatars. Both the human's character and the AI's character individual units are practically the same stat wise, equal. But in terms of processing and thinking, the AI is numerous(potentially infinite) and untiring counterpart. Thats millions of tireless machines vs one tirable human player. Not fair, since considering in the real world, the counterparts are also just the same tirable humans that are also finite as the player is.\par
\par
More over, some games with AI routines do not consider the idea of "thinking things through" when it comes to the AI of characters as it would happen in real life. Not pondering and goofing around like real humans do before they do stuff. Thats where the concept of a delay comes into place.\line\line The delay simply makes the AI actually calculate its actions and how to do them at non-frequent intervals. Or in other words, the AI does intentional pauses in the way of processing every 30 frames instead of every 1 frame.\line\line It pauses both for the purpose of performance to keep AI managable to calculate at a constant rate for potentially thousands of characters and for the sake of "humanizing" the AI characters by making them *think* before they *act* through a delay and thus making AI fair to compensate the infinite numbers and simultaneous operation. Its a skipping delay which adjust to be longer the more chaos/usual activity there is.\line\line This allows for the gameplay interaction with NPCs and other AI characters/systems to occur at a more fair setting and make them a bit more believable, goofy and relatable, when it takes a while for the AI driven NPCs to figure out what to do next or simply do something that is normally irrational due to a threatening fight-or-flight situation. \par
\par
As a game, a bit of equalizing on for the players favor is needed by making the AI a bit more slower than what the player is capable of handling, since machines can be made to respond at ridiculously fast intervals, but as that is unfair, they can also be evened out to be more in line with the player's reflexes and then make them even more slower, to compensate for their infinite numbers and overwhelming power of working all at once.\par
\par
\line\b Long term directives, research, evolution, growth:\par
\b0\par
Or in other words: What is alive in us right now and how to make life more wonderful?\line\line Basically, paradigms are mostly concerned with sustainability, but humans didn't stop there, they wanted to surprass sustainability. The current systems were not as good as they could be and there were some exciting avenues of potential that were too great to not pursue, hence the will to grow and evolve, from which the long term directive, 'How to make life more wonderful', stems from.\par
\par
That particular question/directive is very broad and it is so because practically everything can be attributed to it, from problem solving to upgrading to new capabilities or opening doors to more variety in every aspect of human lives. 'Whats alive in us' asks how are you feel right now, what need is that feeling pointing to and evoke a thought of possibility to do something about that need, whether it has been met or unmet.\line\line If its met, then finding strategies to giving gratitude or celebrating would be an action that happens as a result, which could explore old, new or combined ways of doing that. Evolution would happen.\line\par
If its unmet, then actions might take place to find strategies to meet that need, which prompts exploration into old methods, new methods or combined methods to do that. Again, evolution happens either way.\par
\line Now, from an ingame implementation perspective, this is something that happens by itself as a result of all the above systems, so this is not implemented itself as a concrete component. They just... happen. Automatically. \par
\par
The next part about memories/data will explain in detail how this happens.\line\line\par
\b Accumulating Memories/Data:\b0\line\par
As AI driven characters, NPCs, go about trying to meet their needs or celebrating their satisfaction, they accumulate memories/data. These memories influence strategies to meet needs/celebrate satisfaction.\par
\par
Anything, and I mean ANYTHING, anyone ever does is to meet needs/celebrate satisfaction.\par
\par
So during the strategy to meet the need/celebrate, the AI will try default strategies that are most "normal" for that specific character type (a human being, fish, alien...). During the performance of these strategies for a need, they'll try to find a source, which basically tells the AI to go from A to B. If there is something on the route that impedes or eases this mission in a noticable, significant way, that will be remembered and saved as a memory.\line\line The way something is remember is based on the AI doing the analysis on the spot to identify the source using its senses. This means it might guess and attribute something as a facilitator or a threat, altering the strategy to meet a specific need using this memory fragment to abuse the facilitator or evade/remove the threat with whatever means it has.\par
\par
This means the AI is not "perfect". It can and will guess the cause of something wrongly by accident, causing goofy behaviour at times. This is intentional and it models real life, since there is a huge problem when people do something, but fail at it, but guess the real reason wrongly because they didn't have the means to see the real root cause of a failure.\par
\par
Memories can be passed on from one NPC to another via communication, and thus they can influence each other to be more smarter in meeting needs as a society/community. As in real life, individuals may act on their own less intelligently than groups that work together. \par
\par
Having small things like these happen like this and reveal to the player how the AI works, it can be very powerful in teaching/revealing how humans beings really operate in the real world and understand NVC much faster and better. Thus also change the world.\par
\par
It also opens up the gameplay mechanic and purpose for actual schools/learning centers/education to happen both for the player, the NPCs around them and all the implications that brings.\par
\par
\par
\b Needs and Strategies:\line\par
\b0 Needs are finite and clearly identifiable. Some needs may not be present on some lifeforms/systems, if looking at the difference between a human and a fish or an ant or an alien beast, and some needs ultimately stem from a single need in an indirect way (need for power/freedom may really be just an extension for a need for safety... in some cases. Not all. But in some.)\line\par
Generally, human needs are as follows:\par
\par
\pard{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sl276\slmult1 Subsistence (food, water, air, warmth)\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Safety/Protection\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Freedom/Power/Participation/Access\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Growth/Learning/Advancement/Creation/Variety\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Relaxation/Leisure/Fun\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Meaning/Identity/Belonging\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Equality/Respect/Understanding\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Love/Giving/Affection/Care\par
\pard\sl276\slmult1\par
The needs overlap and need multiple words to laser focus their accurate meaning, but these are pretty much all there are. They can expressed in many ways and different words, but the needs are always the same and always finite. English language can be poor at times due to things like subject to interpretation, lack of accurate words, limits of individual words to describe a single abstract things properly... etc.\line\line Strategies are HOW these needs are met. There are practically infinite ways needs can be met, so they change with the situation so rapidly and are often too limited to encompass every way a need can be met, so its pointless to bother defining them. Instead, only vague strategy lead ins are needed. \par
\par
Like if an NPC is hungry, they need compatible fuel for their system to meet that need. What that fuel is, where is it, whats between the NPC and the fuel are completely situational and those the AI will figure out as it sets out on a mission to meet that need.\line\line As an opposing example, lets take the need for safety. Be inside a bunker? Have guards around you? Equip forcefields/armor? Be out of harms way? Not have enemies? It could be literally anything.\line\par
For this reason, vague but clear strategy kickstarters are needed to let the AI know "naturally" what to look for and how to know it when it finds it. So for the need for safety, "knowing that the AI Character can relax without care and harm" could be more appropriate.\par
\par
It should also be mentioned that strategies can come in different qualities. Some strategies are worse than other, while some are better than others. \par
\par
The reason is that strategies can meet and unmeet needs at the same time, depending on the strategy and the logic... or I'd call it brainwashing... behind it:\line\par
Some strategies can be absolute shite and unmeet many, if not all needs. Worst.\par
Other strategies can meet one need, but unmeet many others. Bad.\par
Some strategies seem like they will meet needs, but they actually do nothing. Meh.\par
Some stragies may meet multiple needs, but unmeet another one. Flawed.\par
Some strategies can meet one need and that one need alone. Good.\par
Some strategies can meet multiple needs at once, with no downsides at all. Best.\par
\par
The spectrum is very wide. In an abundant world, all needs can be met equally, so the last two options on that list are the only ones that should be happening. Conditioning, corrupted core memories, fear, rewards and punishment can cause suicidal behaviour that are in line with the higher items on that list.\par
\par
Some strategical models, such as Power Over People, aka domination structures, punitive justice and violent culture, will favor the top 4, especially the highest one. Meanwhile, Power With People, aka NVC, restorative justice and compassionate culture will favor the bottom 2. The game can also highlight the difference the two and encourage the latter for a better world IRL.\par
\b\line\line Day to day routines:\line\par
\b0 Ingame, there may be cities, stations, towns, ships, camps, nomads... for humans, for animals, for aliens... where the "citizens" go about their days meeting needs and celebrating satisfations.\par
\par
The most basic needs, like eating, drinking and sleeping would be fairly simple. The AI character detects that it wants to meet that need, so it will go to a source from where it could get what it needs or get information where such a source might exist. After a location is confirmed, they'd simply locomote there in the easiest way that is available to them and meet their need from the source directly.\line\line There may be so called extra "transit points", like obstacles, facilitators, preparation steps, modifiers based on other needs (do it safely, do it while also getting some for someone else to give), memory modifiers (this is dangerous, avoid. this source was better than that one.) and such that make the mission sometimes more or less straightforward.\par
\par
More abstract needs, like meaning, love, growth, power, safety and such rely more heavily on memories get them met. Since birth/spawning, certain default strategies are encoded within the DNA of each character archetype that give a vanilla, pre-defined set of memories to know how to meet the needs that also have an affinity of feeling "natural", although those can be overwritten, supressed, corrupted, forgotten or improved... for better... or worse.\par
\par
\par
\b Teaching/Educating/Influencing/Changing the AI:\b0\par
\par
Essentially, NPCs, AI Characters can "communicate" with one another to share, propagate and spread knowledge in the form of memory/data exchange wirelessly. Memories are stored as fragments of concrete informational fact or an instruction, so in action they have a real effect that make up of how AI characters behave, especially after such information sharing interactions that are done to meet a need for growth or a similar abstact need or as a transit point while meeting another need, be it simple or abstract.\par
\par
Other ways of communication can be data terminals with information stored on them, advertisements, posters, television, radio, signs, warnings, schools, information propagation events hosted by individuals to another individual or a crowd in an organized or non-organized setting.\par
\par
These are some if not all methods where data/memory exchange can happen and thus learning/influence can occur. Also for better... or worse. Wink wink.\par
\par
\par
\b NPC dormancy:\b0\line\line During ingame, it is a desirable trait for the game to run smooth, simplify interactions for a more enjoyable play experience and reduce performance load as much as possible.\par
\line Simulating an AI character is heavy stuff. \par
\par
For PW, far-away NPCs are shutdown and their AI is mostly inactive, except for the possible meta implications that could influence things happening in the universe that in turn, could influence the player's current location somehow.\par
\par
Closer in, in the background over a 10km range from the player, AI are loaded, but greatly simplified and abstract. They're lightweight.\line\line Below 10km, but above 1km, AI are loaded, visually simplified, but have more processing active than the ones beyond 10km range. They're mediumweight.\line\par
Below 1km range, AI are fully loaded, both in accurate visual representation and system full systems, only reduced by the Chaos System where applicable. Heavyweight.\par
\par
Next up, there may be areas that have increasingly more NPCs and AIs in heavyweight, mediumweight and lightweight modes. Starting from only one person besides you on a desolate rock in the middle of nowhere, to a small camp with some wildlife around, to a town with some action in the view and some in the distance, up to a megacity central, with lots of NPCs on screen and in the background.\par
\line A way to reduce that load with an ingame solution would be greatly appreciated. One of these, are a home-pod. They can come in various sizes from 1-man to 10-man pods, where characters can go inside, and get most of their basic needs met without leaving. If the pod is destroyed, the character inside of it will pop out. The pods can satisfy some needs (a bed can be a pod, which only satisfies sleep needs) or it can be pod that satisfies many needs (apartment: food, water, sleep, entertainment, safety, community hub, etc).\par
\par
These pods allow NPCs within the 10km radius of the player to go dormant.\par
\par
They still exist, but they're greatly simplified, as they're not visible, physics ragdoll is offline, pathfinding is offline, active thinking is mostly offline, active need meeting/celebration is offline and so on.\line\line They can also be convinient for gameplay purposes to manage NPCs more simply and protect them, make sure their needs are met within a compact all-encompassing system for long-term sustainability, which is greatly important for players venturing on adventure far away from home, but still have an intention to return for various reasons.\par
\par
However, the pods cannot act as clowncars to shove ridiculous amounts of NPCs within a very small space, since all that goes out the window if they are destroyed, blasting out hundreds if not thousands of NPCs that suddenly go all active at once from a single point.\par
\par
The pods only allow as many inhabitants as the size of the pod itself is. The size is naturally dictated by the systems and materials that are required to build it and also the storage space for each character and the need-satisfying goods, like water and food that are stored in a pod for continued operation and enjoyable stay.\par
